the team at pugs is excited to simplify  the development of  their conversational experience using  dialogflow cx  stick around to see it all in action  hi i'm priyanka vergaria and in this  episode of deconstructing chat bots  we will help our friends at pugs create  a conversational experience for their  customer support  using dialogflow cs before we dive into  the console though  let's lay out our use case requirements  first  we know that most of their user requests  are to order dog shoes  so it makes sense to start with the  order flow first  in this flow we want to collect order  information from the user  including the size and the color of the  shoes and then present a confirmation  if the user confirms we end the flow and  if they want to edit the order  then we gather the new size and color  from them and then place the order  now that we know what we are building  let's head to dialogflow cx console the  easiest way is to get there from the  documentation  create a new project make sure you  enable billing and the dialogflow api  then we go back into the dialogflow cx  console and click on the project we just  created  it prompts us to create an agent that's  exactly what we want to do  we name it select the location where we  want it  it and the time zone and the language  and we're good to create now notice that  within a project we can create  multiple agents we have our default  start flow  with our start page which comes with the  default welcome intent  so let's go ahead and test it at this  point  all it does is greets the user we want  to create a transition route  from the start page that leads to the  order flow  create new intent let's name it and add  some training phrases  let's add some basic ones such as i  would like to place an order  but we also need to add some specific  phrases with color  let's add i want to buy green shoes for  my pub  as soon as we add that that notice that  the color parameter  automatically gets created with sis dot  color entity type  and this color parameter will be stored  in the session for us to access in other  pages  also since the user has already provided  the color  our agent will not ask for that  information  providing a more natural experience  now let's add another example with the  size  i want to get large shoes for my husky  now we see that the large is not  automatically identified as a parameter  because size is unique to pugs use case  so let's fix that but before we do that  add a transition to new page  where we will collect information and  save it  let's go into manage entity types and  then create  new name it size and add small  medium large size in there with some  synonyms  now while we are on the manage tab let's  go into the intent to see if we can add  our size parameter now  add one or two more examples with both  sides and color to add some variety  let's head over to our collect  information page and add the entry  dialog  to let the user know that we will  collect some more information now  then add the parameter size is a  required parameter and if a user does  not provide it  we want to prompt them with the  fulfillment here  we repeat the same for color as well  with that  we are ready to define our transition  routes from this page  and in this case we don't need to  trigger an intent all we need  is to make sure our parameters are  filled in  so we look for the condition to match  page.paramster status  equals final once all the parameters are  defined  and collected which means both size  and color in this case that's when the  status will turn to final  this is also where we can match other  conditions such as  if the parameter parameter.colors equals  red  then send to a sorry page because that  color is not available in the inventory  once the status equals final condition  is met we would route the user to the  new confirmation page  in the confirmation page let's first add  the entry dialog to share  what they have selected to buy we can  access the parameters  in the session by using the dollar sign  we don't have  any parameter to add for this page for  the transition  routes we want to have a yes and a no  route  for yes path let's create a new intent  and call it confirmation.yes and provide  a few training phrases that indicate  yes save the intent and then add the  transition page  as success do the same thing for no  route  success page is easy at this point all  we need to do  is add a nice entry dialog for the  transition route add true in the  condition  to force and select end flow as the  transition page  now the failure page we want to see if  the user wants to change their selection  or they just don't want to buy today for  that we add the entry dialog  and then add the transition routes if  they say no  we just want to add a goodbye page quite  simple  but if they say yes then we send them  back to the start of the flow to make  their color  and size selections now let's test it  that doesn't quite work and that's  because the parameters are already  filled we need to clear up the  parameters before the state  changes to the start page and we do that  in the parameter presets in the yes  intent transition  of the failure page let's test it again  and we see that the flow is  all set all right so that was a lot  we created the entire order flow from  scratch by creating a bunch of pages  we also learned how intense parameters  and entities work in conjunction with  the pages  i am very excited for the next episode  where we will extend the pugs  conversational experience  further by adding multiple flows until  then  i look forward to seeing your thoughts  on this episode in the comments below  you
 

